#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063998 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064252 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e05385-7cb6-5f21-b0d8-a7641d912d5b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function"}] :question/id #uuid "121d5f8c-3b38-5c75-aec1-646dc7d2f2f8" :question/math-academy-id "e-14719" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{2-3x}{4+5x}$ and the point $P\\left(-1,-5\\right)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ determine the slope of $y=f^{-1}(x)$ at the point $\\left(-5,-1\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(-1, -5).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2-3x}{4+5x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{22}{(4+5x)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =-22\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(-1, -5)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(-5, -1)$ lies on $y=f^{-1}(x).$ \n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(-5) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(-5))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-22)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{22}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063999 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064251 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4270c31c-80f1-5b5d-bca8-e16a47206c2b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function From a Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "d113528f-a3ff-5f28-b071-bcf355a36b45" :question/math-academy-id "e-14720" :question/problem "![](images/8f/8fb287885c948b7e563b5fa878dd204dacbedea311490c4573aa883bff992662.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ and its inverse $y=f^{-1}(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(2, 0)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$\nThe point $B$ has coordinates $(0,2)$ and lies on $y=f^{-1}(x).$ If $f'(2)=-\\dfrac{3}{2},$ what is $(f^{-1})'(0)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B \\,(0,2)$ lies on $f^{-1},$ we have $f^{-1}(0) = 2.$ We're also told that $f'(2)=-\\dfrac{3}{2}.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064000 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064253 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b492068c-2bd2-5611-baab-780959d24c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function Using a Table"}] :question/id #uuid "9e531872-846f-5e75-97c0-dd2db3515558" :question/math-academy-id "e-14721" :question/problem "The following table gives the values of $f(x), f'(x),g(x),$ and $g'(x)$ at some points. Find $(f^{-1}\\cdot g)'(1)$ of the product of functions.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | $f'(x)$ | $g(x)$ | $g'(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $-3$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $3$ | $5$ |" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse $f^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(f^{-1})'(x) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function $f^{-1}(x)g(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(f^{-1}\\cdot g)'(x)=\\dfrac{g(x)}{f'(f^{-1}(x))}+g'(x)f^{-1}(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n(f^{-1}\\cdot g)'(1) =\\dfrac{g(1)}{f'(f^{-1}(1))}+g'(1)f^{-1}(1).\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain $f^{-1}(1)=2,$ $g(1)=-3,$ $g'(1)=1,$ and $f'(2)=-1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(1) & =\\frac{g(1)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))}+{g}^{′}(1){f}^{-1}(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{{f}^{′}(2)}+1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{-1}+2 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064001 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064254 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2a80aa-7882-50eb-bfe0-c0eeab72161d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Derivative Using Properties of Inverse Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "d38ac709-38e8-51a1-8e08-14913c77639c" :question/math-academy-id "e-14722" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x) =3e^{x}-1.$ Given that $y = f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $(f^{-1})'(2).$" :question/worked-solution "To determine $(f^{-1})'(2),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\left(f^{-1}\\right)'(2) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(2))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of $f^{-1}(2).$ To do this, we solve the equation $f^{-1}(2) = x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(2) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =2 \\\\ 3{e}^{x}-1 & =2 \\\\ 3{e}^{x} & =3 \\\\ {e}^{x} & =1 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f^{-1}(2) = 0.$\n\nNow, we calculate $f'(0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =3{e}^{x}-1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =3{e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3{e}^{0} \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479132 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064251 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4270c31c-80f1-5b5d-bca8-e16a47206c2b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function From a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "246e1b2f-6c69-5623-8546-f8f0f3b9f05b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478615} {:answer/id #uuid "2bdc9d8e-a8b0-5a1f-a275-4f9721c494f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478628} {:answer/id #uuid "3ebda5a4-ae46-5937-b31f-c78480be0131" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478647} {:answer/id #uuid "8f02771c-0941-5ad8-b645-f5b0bd475cd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478785} {:answer/id #uuid "b5ff3de9-2ac1-527c-8bea-2348693c73ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478853}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "246e1b2f-6c69-5623-8546-f8f0f3b9f05b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478615} :answer-field/id #uuid "525e14af-3d5d-5768-99db-cfa31e6ebfe1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478990}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d295fd4d-8093-5953-9c94-5f7f1a143168" :question/math-academy-id "q-110128" :question/problem "![](images/89/89dee9fffb22501189ac9f972dc4bcf7e2ed3602ef000b8ef01f04ddccd2e2eb.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a piecewise linear function $y=f(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(3,3)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $(3,3)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(3)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $(3,3)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(3)=3.$\nThe slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ for $x\\in [0,6]$ is $\\frac{1}{3}$, hence, ${f}^{′}(3)=\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(3) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(3))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233131 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064253 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b492068c-2bd2-5611-baab-780959d24c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function Using a Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b099fca-24ea-5ced-85d0-937df56f7086" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478645} {:answer/id #uuid "61702aec-ec4f-5953-a964-c69981cc8775" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478700} {:answer/id #uuid "837fbf4e-1f0c-58a0-b94d-a38789af2130" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478759} {:answer/id #uuid "99286d9a-1709-5463-8a09-24f3fc27d097" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478811} {:answer/id #uuid "c72d93cc-719b-57d5-9e3f-2f31832fa402" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478878}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "837fbf4e-1f0c-58a0-b94d-a38789af2130" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478759} :answer-field/id #uuid "484e8ff3-0366-5801-ac8d-a4625e5b7f52" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478985}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9e508817-6e98-5a61-86b6-1bdfe91061a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-110234" :question/problem "The following table gives the values of functions $f(x)$ and ${f}^{′}(x)$ at some points. Find $({f}^{-1})^{′}(4).$\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $-2$ | $1$ | $4$ |\n| $1$ | $4$ | $-2$ |" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(4)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(4)=1$ and ${f}^{′}(1)=-2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(4) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247101 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064253 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b492068c-2bd2-5611-baab-780959d24c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function Using a Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25ac83ce-e459-55b8-aabe-83070cf29bc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{31}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244648} {:answer/id #uuid "5d25fab6-2a2a-5dab-bc43-2ac9fb58e1b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{46}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245136} {:answer/id #uuid "6ae1482e-9e5d-53eb-a692-6f23e14d6064" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245260} {:answer/id #uuid "cb5ed946-2a28-561a-8922-89e37b31ab97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246124} {:answer/id #uuid "d397cd55-48b1-5c07-9f19-405352176bf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{28}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246197}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25ac83ce-e459-55b8-aabe-83070cf29bc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{31}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244648} :answer-field/id #uuid "32533013-ed25-57c7-94a4-9a3dd6af2a19" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246630}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1d3c2c96-95a0-5706-9edb-c677d76924c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-110272" :question/problem "The following table gives the values of $f(x),{f}^{′}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points. Find $({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(4)$ of the product of functions.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $6$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $5$ |\n| $3$ | $4$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $-3$ |" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function ${f}^{-1}(x)g(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(x)=\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}+{g}^{′}(x){f}^{-1}(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(4)=\\frac{g(4)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))}+{g}^{′}(4){f}^{-1}(4).\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(4)=3,$ $g(4)=-1,$ ${g}^{′}(4)=5,$ and ${f}^{′}(3)=-2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(4) & =\\frac{g(4)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))}+{g}^{′}(4){f}^{-1}(4) \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{{f}^{′}(3)}+5\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{-2}+15 \\\\  & =\\frac{31}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479075 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064253 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b492068c-2bd2-5611-baab-780959d24c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function Using a Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f66e96d-6782-582b-91de-1a05ef13ce2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{29}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478648} {:answer/id #uuid "44e8533d-7dac-5ba2-978b-55318c3e93f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478657} {:answer/id #uuid "59bfce54-2237-51cf-8a25-f36803b18cb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478684} {:answer/id #uuid "a31f2089-914e-52b4-894d-cc7b35421de1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{29}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478827} {:answer/id #uuid "fdad0167-4721-5b03-9ba3-f3128bcdab9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{19}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478957}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f66e96d-6782-582b-91de-1a05ef13ce2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{29}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478648} :answer-field/id #uuid "35a15c29-4f1a-540c-8233-d86ef79a3897" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478979}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "30cea08b-c33e-58ec-a947-197b4401903e" :question/math-academy-id "q-110287" :question/problem "The following table gives the values of $f(x),{f}^{′}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points. Find $({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(3)$ of the product of functions.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $1$ | $-4$ | $-2$ | $5$ |\n| $-2$ | $3$ | $-6$ | $-1$ | $-4$ |" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function ${f}^{-1}(x)g(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(x)=\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}+{g}^{′}(x){f}^{-1}(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(3)=\\frac{g(3)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(3))}+{g}^{′}(3){f}^{-1}(3).\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(3)=-2,$ $g(3)=-2,$ ${g}^{′}(3)=5,$ and ${f}^{′}(-2)=-6.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(3) & =\\frac{g(3)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(3))}+{g}^{′}(3){f}^{-1}(3) \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{{f}^{′}(-2)}+5\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{-6}-10 \\\\  & =-\\frac{29}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233133 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064254 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2a80aa-7882-50eb-bfe0-c0eeab72161d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Derivative Using Properties of Inverse Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1049ad2b-d3c5-598f-a813-b594700c836e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{15}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478580} {:answer/id #uuid "2b3ce2a3-15e3-5f28-8cd5-6e81df3d84f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478625} {:answer/id #uuid "84826b57-c122-587c-baf5-141a650be923" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478762} {:answer/id #uuid "85d2c120-3987-58fb-b1a8-1cdfdf73f75a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478765} {:answer/id #uuid "fbeb8da5-4868-5726-bf1c-39c8f81f6da3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478953}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84826b57-c122-587c-baf5-141a650be923" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478762} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d842570-384d-5f9a-8934-5fc8f5ec2231" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478974}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cad2b776-429a-5d87-afc3-be9a913d2b9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-110328" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=3x+\\sqrt{x}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2).$" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(2).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(2)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(2) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =2 \\\\ 3x+\\sqrt{x} & =2 \\\\ \\sqrt{x} & =2-3x \\\\ x & =4-12x+9{x}^{2} \\\\ 9{x}^{2}-13x+4 & =0 \\\\ (9x-4)(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{4}{9},\\,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nChecking the solutions, we notice that only $x=\\frac{4}{9}$ satisfies the equation $3x+\\sqrt{x}=2.$ Therefore, we discard $x=1$ and conclude that ${f}^{-1}(2)=\\frac{4}{9}.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(\\frac{4}{9}),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =3x+\\sqrt{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =3+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{4}{9}) & =3+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{(\\frac{4}{9})}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{4}{9}) & =3+\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{4}{9}) & =\\frac{15}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(\\frac{4}{9})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275185 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064252 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e05385-7cb6-5f21-b0d8-a7641d912d5b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a45d87b-807c-503a-b228-c3be7151a506" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272480} {:answer/id #uuid "a55c0552-3d4d-5bbb-af7a-514b512a1c1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273819} {:answer/id #uuid "b90b85b9-8913-5641-851b-1a980b290794" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273990} {:answer/id #uuid "c560404b-1b09-5cb7-ac10-8cbecd5bf80b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274099} {:answer/id #uuid "f932c329-a1a5-5eb4-a2a1-425e66154f8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274573}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b90b85b9-8913-5641-851b-1a980b290794" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273990} :answer-field/id #uuid "c49d6c9e-2557-5663-b1ec-a164fc220c5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274978}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "55ad62a1-4387-5fad-a8b0-2b49bf34cf69" :question/math-academy-id "q-14770" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}+4x+10$ and the point $P(0,10)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ determine the slope of $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ at the point $(10,0).$" :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(0,10).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}+4x+10 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{2}{3}x+4 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =4\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(0,10)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(10,0)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(10) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(10))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233516 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064252 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e05385-7cb6-5f21-b0d8-a7641d912d5b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29f4747e-8569-594d-9e01-6f95ff1bf175" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478624} {:answer/id #uuid "676f26c7-4ab2-5836-869b-b435bda6478d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478711} {:answer/id #uuid "809ed5d0-fd32-504a-bc8a-1e2f04cef67d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478751} {:answer/id #uuid "c253d35f-d637-5a2e-867a-5eb95c510123" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478872} {:answer/id #uuid "ca6374d3-9008-519f-8e9e-84d5bbdcb086" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478882}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "676f26c7-4ab2-5836-869b-b435bda6478d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478711} :answer-field/id #uuid "3284a90b-68b7-5de8-b142-fabe6295ab56" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478975}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a3c782b0-333e-5ef5-97b8-c1749c597dee" :question/math-academy-id "q-15640" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=3(x-2)^{3}$ and the point $P(3,3)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$  calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(3).$" :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(3,3).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =3(x-2)^{3} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =9(x-2)^{2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =9\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(3,3)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(3,3)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(3) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(3))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479102 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064252 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e05385-7cb6-5f21-b0d8-a7641d912d5b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48f99d44-4d8f-5e63-a4f9-ad7e2a143699" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478663} {:answer/id #uuid "4f3cc394-a51e-5e49-9984-a7ac06825005" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478673} {:answer/id #uuid "56a8ed7c-03cd-521a-bb2f-1546988bfe36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478679} {:answer/id #uuid "742bbfa3-afc2-5d30-96a9-b323199b849c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478729} {:answer/id #uuid "7b4ebe60-62a8-5574-8302-183ff5c9dbe0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478742}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48f99d44-4d8f-5e63-a4f9-ad7e2a143699" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478663} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8dff0b2-a40e-5488-9228-977e8100365b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479029}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "854b6ede-01ab-595a-a1e4-df6af7f40d5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-15649" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{5}{8}\\sqrt{x}$ and the point $P(4,\\frac{5}{4})$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ determine the slope of $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ at the point $(\\frac{5}{4},4).$" :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(4,\\frac{5}{4}).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{5}{8}\\sqrt{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{5}{16\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{5}{32}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(4,\\frac{5}{4})$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(\\frac{5}{4},4)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(\\frac{5}{4}) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(\\frac{5}{4}))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{5}{32})} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{5}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275351 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064252 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e05385-7cb6-5f21-b0d8-a7641d912d5b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73a12da4-8719-5cdd-b2c8-74b0f9406bd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273376} {:answer/id #uuid "8e5d0b75-18cd-5b42-9286-53a7ac0e38c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273619} {:answer/id #uuid "9ac771f0-6ed4-5304-bba3-e052c9ad26f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273725} {:answer/id #uuid "badb7865-dc6e-512a-8eef-34ab48b9e2eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274007} {:answer/id #uuid "c4fb585c-5a18-5f14-ac28-7363d6443edc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274094}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73a12da4-8719-5cdd-b2c8-74b0f9406bd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273376} :answer-field/id #uuid "dabf6b20-08dd-5a43-96c7-67e44ad6382c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275017}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8440be8d-9ebe-5dfc-9033-4f02294d7b13" :question/math-academy-id "q-15651" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{x-3}}$ and the point $P(7,\\frac{3}{4})$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ determine the slope of $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ at the point $(\\frac{3}{4},7).$" :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(7,\\frac{3}{4}).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{x-3}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{3}{4(x-3)^{3/2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(7) & =-\\frac{3}{32}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(7,\\frac{3}{4})$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(\\frac{3}{4},7)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(\\frac{3}{4}) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(\\frac{3}{4}))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(7)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{3}{32})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{32}{3}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297349 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064251 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4270c31c-80f1-5b5d-bca8-e16a47206c2b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function From a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b6cfa32-6775-5c83-bbef-9147bd202178" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295794}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b6cfa32-6775-5c83-bbef-9147bd202178" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295794} :answer-field/id #uuid "eeb3a075-f47e-5cb5-80a2-3e23e6313a6b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297124}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "19309cb7-ace3-5af0-908b-5924987e950f" :question/math-academy-id "q-312578" :question/problem "![](images/e0/e00b5083b25408240fb0eef26c898e237437bf9fe0f7fce11e397b9d7683a1c2.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a piecewise linear function $y=f(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(2,0)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $(0,2)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)?$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $(0,2)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(0)=2.$ \nThe slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ for $x\\in [0,4]$ is $\\frac{1}{2}$, hence, ${f}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is \n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317276 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064251 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4270c31c-80f1-5b5d-bca8-e16a47206c2b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function From a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c699830-6b48-59b4-85fe-4282627a4952" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315466}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c699830-6b48-59b4-85fe-4282627a4952" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315466} :answer-field/id #uuid "794929a7-0728-52c2-b3a4-bcc7e3978c87" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316836}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c15b3ea9-8f3d-5cc5-a69a-f9caee4c79da" :question/math-academy-id "q-342561" :question/problem "![](images/ad/adcee8bddcbbd96e18d63f85f5b9ebf2edc52897cc1ddcca7e377194462f6bda.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ and its inverse $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(-1,0)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ The point $B$ has coordinates $(0,-1)$ and lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ If ${f}^{′}(-1)=3,$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)?$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $B\\,(0,-1)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(0)=-1.$\nWe're also told that ${f}^{′}(-1)=3.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479082 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064251 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4270c31c-80f1-5b5d-bca8-e16a47206c2b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function From a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "001d2f3c-cc4f-5b57-bf9b-9dee9b9d3cae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478562}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "001d2f3c-cc4f-5b57-bf9b-9dee9b9d3cae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478562} :answer-field/id #uuid "d84637eb-2310-5af2-a7e5-b3d8297c7f31" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479042}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3e911702-eb82-5b97-88c2-1d3bfbe6d604" :question/math-academy-id "q-342576" :question/problem "![](images/9b/9bb238610c8f58efb4e0d1c41de0cec2fe2406e0f29e465237560b73cdb3618f.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a piecewise linear function $y=f(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(0,0)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $(0,0)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)?$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $(0,0)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(0)=0.$ \nThe slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ for $x\\in [-1,1]$ is $3$, hence, ${f}^{′}(0)=3.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is \n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479119 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064251 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4270c31c-80f1-5b5d-bca8-e16a47206c2b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function From a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b155602a-a9af-5cac-9ef9-d26df365210e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478845}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b155602a-a9af-5cac-9ef9-d26df365210e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478845} :answer-field/id #uuid "08cb8f7a-3eff-5e61-a8e7-a58164764082" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478962}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b47ad43a-e012-58c2-b33a-c151f7cd3d62" :question/math-academy-id "q-342577" :question/problem "![](images/37/37067c32385be4b652765062bbaf4e413ec87cec2ec6d0d01c6cd5dce64d7d9a.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a piecewise linear function $y=f(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(1,0)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $(0,1)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)?$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $(0,1)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(0)=1.$ \nThe slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ for $x\\in [-1,3]$ is $\\frac{3}{2}$, hence, ${f}^{′}(1)=\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is \n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317285 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064252 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e05385-7cb6-5f21-b0d8-a7641d912d5b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35c47875-1ef5-501d-bbee-a417107b5d82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315433}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35c47875-1ef5-501d-bbee-a417107b5d82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315433} :answer-field/id #uuid "a259aaa2-07f9-5836-91da-9db416d5277d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316924}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e562f9e3-80f6-5a8e-be6e-6fda00e93b68" :question/math-academy-id "q-342584" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and the point $P(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ the slope of $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ at the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ is$\\,${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317288 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064253 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b492068c-2bd2-5611-baab-780959d24c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function Using a Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17f78749-0402-5a44-93c5-6dc18a5b2988" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315256}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17f78749-0402-5a44-93c5-6dc18a5b2988" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315256} :answer-field/id #uuid "62f6bdd9-4210-5afe-bc2f-e04b8e754834" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316780}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cca80ae0-62ef-5413-ba41-4aa5821f7e48" :question/math-academy-id "q-342589" :question/problem "Find $({f}^{-1})^{′}(-1)$ given the following table of values of functions $f(x)$ and ${f}^{′}(x)$ at some points.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $-2$ | $-1$ | $6$ |\n| $4$ | $3$ | $-2$ |\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(-1)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(-1)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(-1))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(-1)=-2$ and ${f}^{′}(-2)=6.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(-1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(-1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479089 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064253 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b492068c-2bd2-5611-baab-780959d24c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function Using a Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1b0ae75-6c13-58b5-926a-902471dc65c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478847}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1b0ae75-6c13-58b5-926a-902471dc65c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478847} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1b8081d-9962-5728-8702-fe7f036192b8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479053}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "518b39e3-bc07-5491-9032-d91dac622690" :question/math-academy-id "q-342595" :question/problem "Find ${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(-1)$ of the product of functions, given the following table of values of $f(x),{f}^{′}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $1$ |\n| $5$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $-2$ |\n\n${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(-1)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function $g(x){f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(x)={g}^{′}(x){f}^{-1}(x)+\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(-1)={g}^{′}(-1){f}^{-1}(-1)+\\frac{g(-1)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(-1))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(-1)=5,$ $g(-1)=-2,$ ${g}^{′}(-1)=1,$ and ${f}^{′}(5)=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(-1) & ={g}^{′}(-1){f}^{-1}(-1)+\\frac{g(-1)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(-1))} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 5+\\frac{(-2)}{{f}^{′}(5)} \\\\  & =5+\\frac{(-2)}{1} \\\\  & =5+(-2) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317299 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064254 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2a80aa-7882-50eb-bfe0-c0eeab72161d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Derivative Using Properties of Inverse Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "069fcbbe-a833-5d2a-87dc-7bdf578f1327" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315152}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "069fcbbe-a833-5d2a-87dc-7bdf578f1327" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315152} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fb4bc15-a2da-5762-850d-38cbd5e5de68" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316849}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "94b649da-ce0f-5be9-8f87-0b0ee7645cbe" :question/math-academy-id "q-342625" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=(x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1).$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0).$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(0).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(0)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(0) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =0 \\\\ (x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(0)=2.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1) \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)+\\frac{x+3}{x-1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-1)+\\frac{2+3}{2-1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =0+5 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064954 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064254 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2a80aa-7882-50eb-bfe0-c0eeab72161d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Derivative Using Properties of Inverse Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "268c70ca-9195-5ed7-a069-2dddcc29c527" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342566} {:answer/id #uuid "787993cd-3298-5ce6-858f-c0975144378a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343279} {:answer/id #uuid "85f9d78e-b30c-534e-a8ad-42ebe5677c35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343389} {:answer/id #uuid "da79b542-167d-552a-a5e9-3a6c9ce02cd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344158} {:answer/id #uuid "f65c1423-e0a4-5ddf-a34e-98c6633665e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344406}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85f9d78e-b30c-534e-a8ad-42ebe5677c35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343389} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b976e39-0610-5ce5-af13-9d1cc55b54c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344680}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3dd725b3-7f44-5fca-9ff0-b43869ecbad5" :question/math-academy-id "q-75986" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-4).$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0).$" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(0).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(0)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(0) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =0 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-4) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe discard $x=0$ since the logarithm is not defined here. Therefore, ${f}^{-1}(0)=5.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(5),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-4) \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-4)+\\frac{x}{x-4} \\\\ {f}^{′}(5) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5-4)+\\frac{5}{5-4} \\\\ {f}^{′}(5) & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366274 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064254 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2a80aa-7882-50eb-bfe0-c0eeab72161d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Derivative Using Properties of Inverse Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b42161f-1560-5a88-b672-16d3a6c105b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366276} {:answer/id #uuid "b9c48b02-063f-5744-be52-d2ccdaf85fa9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366277} {:answer/id #uuid "221a20c8-4569-5827-b5a3-0104b98273d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366278} {:answer/id #uuid "96652cb2-c644-5003-8a9d-a90f516c4ec0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{32\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366279} {:answer/id #uuid "590cda43-c9f1-5ada-b5d9-d47306c771f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366280}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "221a20c8-4569-5827-b5a3-0104b98273d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366278} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5390472-80eb-5d87-8281-9e88c9d4585c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366275}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "67a843ac-f40c-59da-8377-455b0890a108" :question/math-academy-id "q-76108" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={2}^{x}+1.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(5).$" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(5),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(5)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(5))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(5).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(5)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(5) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =5 \\\\ {2}^{x}+1 & =5 \\\\ {2}^{x} & =4 \\\\ {2}^{x} & ={2}^{2} \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(5)=2.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={2}^{x}+1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot {2}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(5) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(5))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345182 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064254 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2a80aa-7882-50eb-bfe0-c0eeab72161d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Derivative Using Properties of Inverse Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0381b6fa-d2c3-5a96-b994-313d6089fff1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342280} {:answer/id #uuid "285b5970-e627-5540-9825-0616aa8b22f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342578} {:answer/id #uuid "76b400f8-9d16-5bfa-8073-5a9772c5e13a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343268} {:answer/id #uuid "936ae9cc-71a8-5b1c-a377-4f7b8354937e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343528} {:answer/id #uuid "f33b22bc-9ce8-5c00-83c4-b16a6248e9d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344376}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0381b6fa-d2c3-5a96-b994-313d6089fff1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342280} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e69cb9d-fd4d-53e2-a61b-5d23ebea3ede" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344741}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f7615d87-ff46-5839-a075-3229075fb3df" :question/math-academy-id "q-76115" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-1}}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1).$" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(1).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(1)=x.$ For $x>\\frac{1}{2},$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(1) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-1}} & =1 \\\\ 1 & =\\sqrt{2x-1} \\\\ 1 & =2x-1 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(1)=1.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-1}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{(2x-1)^{3/2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =-\\frac{1}{(2\\cdot 1-1)^{3/2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
